From 3682b0aa6ce804ccb266c75ad9f5ec024a276db6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HOTTY Agent <hotty@neuroplast.io>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 14:51:15 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] blitz-dom: text controls are as wide as size and cols, as
 tall as rows
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A text input was 300px wide, or the available width when less, whatever
its `size`, and 0 wide at min-content. A textarea was 300px wide unless
it had `cols`, then cols × 0.6em, and rows × 1.2em tall.

Both now count characters as Chromium does
(font_metrics::control_font_metrics). One character is the primary
font's average width (OS/2 xAvgCharWidth), or the advance of `0` when
the font has no average or a full-width one. An input is `size`
characters wide (20 by default), plus the font's widest glyph (its head
bounding box) less one character: the slack Internet Explorer added. A
textarea is `cols` characters wide (20 by default), with no scrollbar
gutter, since Blitz's scrollbars are overlays. That is also the
min-content width: a text control does not shrink to fit, as in browsers.
For Noto Sans and Noto Sans Mono at 16px, every width matches Chromium's
to the pixel.

A row (2 by default, one for an input) is the line height. With
`line-height: normal`, that is the primary font's own (ascent, descent
and line gap), the height Parley lays each line out in, instead of 1.2em,
which cut off the last row in a font with taller metrics.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
